  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "Repair, replace, or replace and re-vent? How we decide in Oshawa",
   "html": "<p>In a newer subdivision the repair-or-replace call is about age and the failed part. In an older Oshawa home there is a third question: what happens to the chimney? A chimney-vented furnace past 20 years is almost always a replacement candidate, and the new furnace will vent out the side wall through PVC. That leaves the chimney serving only the gas water heater, which is often too small a load for a big old flue.</p>"
           "<p>So when we quote a replacement in a wartime or post-war house, the quote includes the venting route, the condensate drain and a plan for the chimney: a properly sized liner for the water heater, or a water heater that vents out the wall too. Skipping that step is how homeowners end up with a new furnace and a damp, crumbling chimney two winters later.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Ignitor, flame sensor, capacitor or pressure switch on any furnace: repair</li><li>Blower, inducer or board on a furnace under 12 years: usually repair</li><li>Major part on a chimney-vented furnace past 20 years: replace and re-vent, with the chimney plan priced</li><li>Cracked heat exchanger or condensation damage in the flue: replace</li></ul>"
           "<p>Our <a href=\"/oshawa/\">Oshawa HVAC page</a> covers the chimney liner question and duct testing in more depth. For error codes, tune-ups and no-heat troubleshooting on any furnace in Ontario, see the province-wide <a href=\"/furnace/repair-maintenance/\">furnace repair and maintenance</a> page.</p>"},
